  start_line=452  header=["Entry / ground", "Admission and exact scope", "Evidence"]  delim_cells=3  data_rows=2  verdict_header=NO
      row  454: ["`knowledge/regression-tally-completeness.md`", "Core ground (the lane's own runner, `tools/mutate-and-run.sh --regression`), not a frontier move. The tally refuses as SETUP when the counted leg statuses do not add up to the case count; `--selftest` runs the extracted tally block under 23 fixed status vectors and a guard stress case (97 \u2192 106 cases). Claim C1 is exploratory: a static-model control met the frozen counter v4 before data", "S087 (re-opened), S093, S094; `.ufo/evidence/round-30/contract-amendment-4.json`"]
      row  455: ["S092 supersedes S059", "Anchor re-pin only. The markdown digest changed and the quoted known issue still stands", "`.ufo/evidence/round-30/sources/S059-bazel-remote-caching.md`"]
  start_line=461  header=["Entry / ground", "Admission and exact scope", "Evidence"]  delim_cells=3  data_rows=1  verdict_header=NO
      row  463: ["`knowledge/executable-self-assessment.md`", "New lane ground (seat ScoutFrontier: not F1\u2013F10, not held core): a standing SCORECARD capability or score row is a claim checked by running the row's own command at the same revision and comparing the stated values; a stale row is corrected by an appended, revision-named statement, never in place. The executable-example sources motivate this local inference; a matching tally is not full behavioral proof. Calibration claim C1 measures it over the 12 frozen rows.", "S055, S095, S096; `.ufo/evidence/round-31/contract.json`; `.ufo/evidence/round-31/c1-measure.py`; `.ufo/evidence/round-31/before/c1-at-base.stdout`"]
  start_line=469  header=["Entry / ground", "Admission and exact scope", "Evidence"]  delim_cells=3  data_rows=1  verdict_header=NO
      row  471: ["`knowledge/runner-tally-audit.md`", "Core ground (the lane's own runner, `tools/mutate-and-run.sh --regression` and its tally selftest harness), not a frontier move (seat ScoutFrontier). A new versioned tool, `tools/tally-audit.py`, audits the runner without editing it: per-leg presence of the tally loop, the tally block against an oracle over every status 0\u2013255 with overcount/undercount stress, and the runner's own harness against a panel of faulty tally blocks. It closes the per-leg item and the eight MutateHead survivors routed since round 30, while the runner stays byte-identical. The measured result is in SCORECARD.md's round-32 block", "S097, S099, S094 (re-opened); `.ufo/evidence/round-32/contract.json`, `.ufo/evidence/round-32/v2/`"]
  start_line=480  header=["Entry / ground", "Admission and exact scope", "Evidence"]  delim_cells=3  data_rows=3  verdict_header=NO
      row  482: ["`knowledge/runner-tally-audit-v2.md`", "Core ground, landed round 33: the four held-out classes that defeated `tools/tally-audit.py` and the v2 answers (runtime vector sweep, every status at every leg, dataflow-identified tally loop, extraction tolerance). Its own header trio already named S097 + S099; this row closes the index gap only", "S097, S099; `tools/tally-audit-v2.py`; `.ufo/evidence/round-33/`"]
      row  483: ["`knowledge/survivor-fixture-minimization.md`", "Frontier F6 attempt (round 29), honest stop: the frozen target was not reached; the note records the survivor-B5 finding and the delta-debugging reading of S024. Index gap only \u2014 no verdict changes", "S024; `.ufo/evidence/round-29/sources/S024-fuzzingbook-Reducer.html`"]
      row  484: ["`knowledge/witness-equivalence-rule.md`", "Core ground, landed round 34: the set-aware witness rule for sealed held-out copies (kill/accept \u00d7 head/vector branches), the rule the round-34 C1 deciding run verified. Header trio added the same round (C2 m1)", "S097, S101; `.ufo/evidence/round-33/c1/c1-measure.py`"]
  start_line=516  header=["Note", "Holds", "Sources"]  delim_cells=3  data_rows=1  verdict_header=NO
      row  518: ["`knowledge/r41-variable-destination-writes.md`", "The two sources that supply the mechanism \u2014 `ast` (S153) and `Path.write_text`'s receiver-as-destination rule (S154) \u2014 and the measured live finding: `tools/repeat-control.py:811` and `:820` resolve to expected-value destinations that the round-40 line matcher cannot connect, both scratch-exempt. Scope is depth-4 resolution through assignment, `os.path.join`, `+`, `/` and f-strings; aliases, closures and non-folding calls are named as unresolved. Claims no universality and no gate coverage. It also records a known false-positive class: a destination whose base is an unbound attribute or a non-folding call is labelled unflagged, and must be read against its base expression.", "S153, S154"]
  start_line=529  header=["Note", "Holds", "Sources"]  delim_cells=3  data_rows=2  verdict_header=NO
      row  531: ["`knowledge/pit-outcome-vocabulary.md`", "PIT's seven outcomes, the No coverage / Survived split, and the run-to-run timeout instability. PIT's code scores a run error as detected while Stryker (held S001) leaves it out of the score, so a score quoted without its outcome classes cannot be compared across the two tools. Assimilated from the shokuknow package and re-grounded this round.", "S155, S156, S158, S001"]
      row  532: ["`knowledge/r42-attribute-and-outflag-destinations.md`", "Two write-destination shapes that v3 misreads: a `self.<attr>` bound to `tempfile.mkdtemp` and read through a same-class helper, and a parameter fed by an argparse `--out` value at every in-module call. The measured live finding is that v3's three unflagged sites are scratch (`tools/context-capture.py:1682`, `:1685`) and operator-named (`tools/registry-replay.py:123`).", "S157, S153, S001, S158"]
  start_line=547  header=["Note", "Holds", "Sources"]  delim_cells=3  data_rows=1  verdict_header=NO
      row  549: ["`knowledge/r43-survivor-resolution-rule.md`", "The K/D/E/U rule the lane applies to a mutant that survives its battery: killed (a reachable-code fixture flips a declared class under the exit-plus-FAIL oracle), dead_code_only (every differing case is a fixture whose discriminating construct is unreachable), equivalent (no differing case in the declared corpus), unresolved (everything else, including run errors and apply errors). Resolves the two round-42 survivors: the outflag mutant killed by F11, the method-fold mutant named dead-code-only rather than relabelled. Records PIT's `EQUIVALENT(true)` convention and why the lane does not adopt its scoring.", "S089, S159, S155, S158"]
  start_line=561  header=["Note", "Holds", "Sources"]  delim_cells=3  data_rows=2  verdict_header=NO
      row  563: ["`knowledge/r8-gtest-order-and-seed.md`", "GoogleTest's own statement that test order is undefined, that `--gtest_shuffle` reveals bad inter-test dependencies, and that the printed random seed (`--gtest_random_seed=SEED`) reproduces an order-related failure; the per-test fixture object as the isolation default", "S256"]
      row  564: ["`knowledge/r8-gtest-primer-independence.md`", "The primer's independence/repeatability requirement, the fixture lifecycle (a fixture is deleted before the next is created; no reuse across tests) and the fatal/nonfatal assertion scope as an upstream instance of the F2 witness-scoped verdict", "S257"]
  start_line=572  header=["Note", "Holds", "Sources"]  delim_cells=3  data_rows=3  verdict_header=NO
      row  574: ["`knowledge/r6-flaky-failures-cluster.md`", "A published abstract states that flaky tests co-occur in clusters with shared root causes (75% of flaky tests belong to a cluster, mean cluster size 13.5); the entry's fixture-craft reading \u2014 controls or survivors drawn from one cluster are one observation \u2014 is labelled inference", "S258"]
      row  575: ["`knowledge/r5-undetected-flaky-failures.md`", "A same-job rerun is both the detection mechanism and its blind spot: undetected flaky failures are 9.8 %-16.3 % of failed pipeline runs (8.8 billion executions, four industrial projects), with up to 3\u00d7 variation between test environments", "S259"]
      row  576: ["`knowledge/r9-false-red-rate.md`", "The publisher's own post states that about 84% of observed pass-to-fail transitions involve a flaky test and that legitimate failures in flaky tests are commonly ignored; the lane's price-the-rerun arithmetic stays out unless a held row states it", "S260"]
  start_line=584  header=["Note", "Holds", "Sources"]  delim_cells=3  data_rows=3  verdict_header=NO
      row  586: ["`knowledge/flaky-aware-verdict-evidence.md`", "Two published statements about what a flaky verdict can rest on: a model over already-recorded outcomes, durations and code history predicts flakiness without added reruns (one industrial system, 100 flaky and 100 non-flaky histories, F1 95.5% from 3 features), and a rerun-based label has a measured budget (at most 5 reruns check at least 82% correctly on 26 Java modules) with order diversity priced below repetition; what a one-run gate verdict is worth is labelled inference", "S261, S262"]
      row  587: ["`knowledge/r8-flakiness-not-static.md`", "A posted (under review) counter-case to code-based flakiness detection: flakiness is not a static property of test code, benchmark shortcuts inflate F1, and the abstract's own caution that the counterfactual dataset is not a benchmark for reuse; the entry states what only an abstract shows", "S263"]
      row  588: ["`knowledge/r8-flaky-cost-baseline.md`", "The price of the branch a rerun cadence chooses between, from one industrial record's abstract: an automatic rerun costs 0.02 cents against $5.67 for a manual investigation of a failed pipeline; the carrier's venue field is wrong and is reported as a carrier defect", "S264"]
  start_line=596  header=["Note", "Holds", "Sources"]  delim_cells=3  data_rows=1  verdict_header=NO
      row  598: ["`knowledge/pbt-generator-pilot.md`", "One generator, `tools/pbt-staged-id.py`, against `tools/staged-id-guard.py` through its CLI only, with an oracle that re-implements the guard's round-43 rules, twelve printed distribution classes, a seed and a case count; measured by a twelve-mutant panel hidden from its author (differential confirmation) against a fixture-replay control; the limits (one gate, no shrinking, a counter blind to a differential generator)", "S269, S270"]
  start_line=604  header=["Note", "Holds", "Sources"]  delim_cells=3  data_rows=2  verdict_header=NO
      row  606: ["`knowledge/fixture-shrinking-pilot.md`", "One shrinker, `tools/shrink-case.py`, over the round-49 generator's killing cases for mutants of `tools/staged-id-guard.py`: units are registry lines, staged lines and map entries; a kill is the round-49 oracle passing on the pristine guard and failing on the mutant; delta debugging to a single-unit fixpoint with verdict caching; every guard run counted in `runs`; measured by a frozen counter that re-checks sub-case, kill and 1-minimality itself on 12 public and 8 hidden mutants and compares the stated cost with a run log the shrinker can reach (the cost was re-counted from outside by a wave-2 trace, and the counter's forgery routes are disclosed in `corpus/lessons/round-50.md`); the limits (line-level units, tiny cases, one gate, a greedy loop meets the same bar, B5 not discharged)", "S271, S022, S024"]
      row  607: ["`knowledge/delta-debugging-minimization-contract.md`", "Admitted from the shokuknow package (staged S167, re-grounded as S271): a reduced fixture states its 1-minimality witness (a removal that loses the failure) and its cost in test runs per reduction \u2014 57 runs for the source's 896-line reduction, 139 only as the case study's total (two reductions summed is an inference); what is new against the held S024 restatement", "S271, S024"]
  start_line=615  header=["id", "ground", "candidate sources", "status", "next falsifiable move (before \u2192 after)"]  delim_cells=5  data_rows=10  verdict_header=NO
