Reading: the token set is matched by EXACT string in the data cell, so a
verdict written `KILLED` or **KILLED** or `killed` is not a verdict to
this reader: the row is refused with `no-verdict`. The HEADER cell, by
contrast, goes through norm_cell() and is matched case-insensitively
and formatting-insensitively. The asymmetry is undeclared in the plan.

==============================================================================
(h) hostile-side backstop — the stray-verdict rule where tables() misses
==============================================================================
H1 hidden 2nd verdict table, THREE-hyphen delimiter              verdict_tables=2 badrows=12 ['out-of-set:331:C001', 'out-of-set:332:C002']
H2 hidden 2nd verdict table, ONE-hyphen delimiter (GFM-legal)    verdict_tables=1 badrows=12 ['stray-verdict:331:| C001 | a | b | KILLED ', 'stray-verdict:332:| C002 | a | b | KILLED ']
H3 hidden 2nd verdict table inside a blockquote                  verdict_tables=1 badrows=12 ['stray-verdict:331:> | C001 | a | b | KILLE', 'stray-verdict:332:> | C002 | a | b | KILLE']
H4 hidden 2nd verdict table indented 4 spaces (indented code block) verdict_tables=2 badrows=12 ['out-of-set:331:C001', 'out-of-set:332:C002']
H5 stray pseudo-table row, token as a CODE SPAN                  verdict_tables=1 badrows=0 
H6 stray pseudo-table row, token in BOLD                         verdict_tables=1 badrows=0 
H7 stray pseudo-table row, token in lower case                   verdict_tables=1 badrows=0 

Reading: the stray-verdict rule is the backstop for every class where
tables() declines the table (H2/H3/H4/H5/H6/H7 all yield badrows>0, so
the `ids` conjunct is false and the state is refused). The exact-string
token match is the same in the table rule and the stray rule, so a
backticked/bolded/lower-case token is invisible to BOTH (H5/H6/H7).

